WEBVTT

1
00:00:14.224 --> 00:00:17.485
The day my life changed began like any other day.

2
00:00:18.146 --> 00:00:21.768
I stayed home and watched our slave Sofia prepare lunch.

3
00:00:22.288 --> 00:00:25.510
Pompeii was a rich city near the mountain Vesuvius.

4
00:00:26.110 --> 00:00:29.052
Most families in Pompeii had three to five slaves.

5
00:00:30.448 --> 00:00:34.570
Our slaves were treated like family, and Sofia was my best friend.

6
00:00:35.350 --> 00:00:39.912
My mother was at the market selling fish, and my father and brothers were fishing.

7
00:00:40.532 --> 00:00:44.114
A little before noon, Sofia and I took lunch to my mother.

8
00:00:46.214 --> 00:00:52.037
The market was crowded, and I didn't want to stay there, so I said, "Mom, Sofia and I

9
00:00:52.077 --> 00:00:54.498
will take lunch to Father and my brothers."

10
00:00:55.178 --> 00:00:57.719
My mother looked at me and said, "Go.

11
00:00:58.139 --> 00:00:59.540
We're almost finished here."

12
00:01:01.672 --> 00:01:03.012
Sofia and I left.

13
00:01:03.533 --> 00:01:05.773
We were both happy to be free of the city.

14
00:01:06.333 --> 00:01:09.494
We walked through the Gate of Neptune and bowed to the god.

15
00:01:13.375 --> 00:01:15.716
It was a short walk from the gate to the marina.

16
00:01:16.316 --> 00:01:21.918
My father's boat wasn't there, so Sofia and I sat on the dock and soaked our feet in

17
00:01:21.938 --> 00:01:24.118
the cool waters of the Mediterranean.

18
00:01:24.738 --> 00:01:29.640
Sofia shaded her eyes with one hand and suddenly shouted, "There's your father!"

19
00:01:33.484 --> 00:01:39.726
Sofia and I waited on my father and brothers as they ate, while three slaves took the fish

20
00:01:39.846 --> 00:01:40.986
off my father's boat.

21
00:01:41.586 --> 00:01:42.506
"How was the catch?"

22
00:01:42.887 --> 00:01:43.867
I asked my father.

23
00:01:43.887 --> 00:01:45.967
"Fair," he replied.

24
00:01:45.987 --> 00:01:50.749
"It's so hot," I said.

25
00:01:50.769 --> 00:01:53.309
"I don't want to go back to the city."

26
00:01:53.329 --> 00:02:00.111
"Let's take the boat out and go for a swim in the cooler, deeper water," my brother suggested.

27
00:02:00.131 --> 00:02:01.192
"Can we?"

28
00:02:01.512 --> 00:02:02.472
I asked my father.

29
00:02:03.132 --> 00:02:08.754
My father could never say no to me, since I was his youngest and his favorite.

30
00:02:11.334 --> 00:02:13.495
He looked at me and then at my brother.

31
00:02:13.515 --> 00:02:19.937
"Marcus Octavius, you can take Julia and Sofia out, but no fooling around.

32
00:02:20.577 --> 00:02:22.777
I expect you to be back by sunset."

33
00:02:22.797 --> 00:02:25.138
"Thank you, Father," I said.

34
00:02:25.758 --> 00:02:28.619
My brother took us out into the Bay of Naples.

35
00:02:29.159 --> 00:02:31.740
The coastline was beautiful in the sunshine.

36
00:02:35.548 --> 00:02:38.970
My brother dropped the anchor and said, "Let's swim!"

37
00:02:39.590 --> 00:02:42.712
He dove in and then Sofia and I followed.

38
00:02:43.292 --> 00:02:45.974
The water was refreshing and cool.

39
00:02:46.614 --> 00:02:52.958
As we were splashing and playing in the water, we heard a thunderous roar, and the sky became

40
00:02:53.078 --> 00:02:58.121
dark. We couldn't see anything; day had become night.

41
00:02:58.801 --> 00:03:03.844
My brother climbed back into the boat, and he helped Sofia and me get in.

42
00:03:04.604 --> 00:03:09.665
My brother was a good sailor, so he knew where to go even though we couldn't see.

43
00:03:12.206 --> 00:03:15.707
As we got closer to shore, it became harder to breathe.

44
00:03:16.347 --> 00:03:19.888
The air was filled with dust and bad-smelling gases.

45
00:03:20.488 --> 00:03:27.090
We could also see large flaming rocks shooting out of the mountain and a river of red flowing

46
00:03:27.170 --> 00:03:28.050
down its side.

47
00:03:29.911 --> 00:03:32.312
Sofia and I held each other and watched.

48
00:03:32.932 --> 00:03:34.980
My brother said, "It's gone!

49
00:03:35.501 --> 00:03:36.501
It's all gone!

50
00:03:37.101 --> 00:03:41.663
The marina, the city, our home—it's all gone," he cried out.

51
00:03:41.683 --> 00:03:43.264
"How can you tell?"

52
00:03:43.744 --> 00:03:44.204
I asked.

53
00:03:44.224 --> 00:03:48.666
"It all looks so strange, like some awful dream."

54
00:03:48.686 --> 00:03:52.828
"It's gone," he said quietly.

55
00:03:52.848 --> 00:03:59.771
"I use Mount Vesuvius as a guide, but half the mountain is missing, and our city has gone

56
00:03:59.851 --> 00:04:00.311
with it."

57
00:04:00.931 --> 00:04:03.012
I held Sofia tighter and cried.

58
00:04:03.580 --> 00:04:06.061
It seemed like it was the end of the world.

59
00:04:07.302 --> 00:04:09.663
My brother turned the boat back out to sea.

60
00:04:10.183 --> 00:04:16.886
The next day, we landed in Sorrento with Vesuvius still smoking and flaming in the background.

61
00:04:18.206 --> 00:04:20.767
Our city was gone, but we remained.

62
00:04:21.408 --> 00:04:24.429
My brother married Sofia and continued to fish.

63
00:04:25.069 --> 00:04:30.912
I married a lemon merchant from Sorrento, but I never forgot the last day of Pompeii.

64
00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:36.862
Mount Vesuvius erupted on August 24, 79 AD.

65
00:04:37.862 --> 00:04:42.164
It destroyed Pompeii and killed most of the twenty thousand people who lived there.

66
00:04:43.325 --> 00:04:49.107
The city disappeared, buried under seven to eight meters of dust, rock, and ash.

67
00:04:51.168 --> 00:05:00.072
After 1,700 years Pompeii was rediscovered, and many of its buildings, monuments, and houses were uncovered and restored.

68
00:05:01.408 --> 00:05:03.390
Today it is a famous tourist attraction.

69
00:05:04.892 --> 00:05:08.055
Pompeii is a reminder to us all of the power of nature.
